# Build trees
build/
build-*/

# LaTeX intermediates. The PDF itself IS tracked -- it is the deliverable most
# people will read, and rebuilding it needs a full TeX install.
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.synctex.gz

# Raw field dumps consumed by doc/fig/*.py to make the figures. 21 MB of binary
# that would sit in history forever, and every one is regenerable by re-running
# its case with FIGDUMP=1 (or FIGVOL=1 / FIGVEC=1 for the aorta). The PNGs they
# produce ARE tracked, because doc/m3lb.tex needs them to build.
doc/fig/*.bin
*.ppm

# Stray run outputs
*.vti

# Third-party publisher PDFs. Kept on disk, deliberately not shipped:
# redistributing them is not ours to do.
SomeRefs/
.DS_Store

# Field dumps and animation frames under results/. These are regenerable by
# re-running the case (-field for tgv2d, -fevery for channel3d) and they are
# large: the channel's mid-plane contour dumps alone came to 59 MB against 24 kB
# for the profile tables beside them. The SUMMARY tables in results/ are tracked
# reference data; per-node field data is not.
results/*/field_*.dat
results/*/anim_*
results/I_channel/*_contour.dat
# Same rule for the Zhou et al. benchmarks: the raw float32 slices behind the
# contour figures are per-node data. The summary tables (conduction.dat,
# plate.dat, natural.dat, lid.dat) and the 1-D profile and centreline dumps
# beside them ARE tracked -- they are what the comparisons are read off.
results/J_zhou_thermal/*_temp.dat
results/J_zhou_thermal/*_ux.dat
results/J_zhou_thermal/*_uy.dat

# Rendered animations. Regenerable from doc/fig/*.py given a dump, and a dump
# is itself ignored -- so these are two steps from anything tracked. doc/m3lb.pdf
# is the tracked binary here because it is the deliverable; a video of a
# demonstrator is not.
doc/video/
# Same rule one directory down: results/*/png/ is what results/*/render_*.py
# writes from an (already ignored) results/*/anim_* dump. 129 frames of
# mhd_sphere came to 4.9 MB of PNG. The SCRIPT is tracked, its output is not.
results/*/png/
results/*/*.mp4
results/*/*.gif
# ParaView output. Same rule again: 12.6 MB per frame at N = 64 for six arrays,
# so a 129-frame run is 1.6 GB. The CASE writes it (-vti) and check_vti.py
# validates it; neither the .vti nor the .pvd is reference data.
results/*/vti/
GPU/vti/
# Bytecode cache. results/ now holds Python renderers, and importing one writes
# a .pyc beside it.
__pycache__/
GPU/build*/

# CSF3 job output. GPU/csf3/*.sub write field planes under runs/ and SLURM
# writes its logs into the submit directory; neither belongs in history.
runs/
rbcold-*.log
cmbench-*.log

# Local run directories from demonstrator/rb_high_ra. Field planes plus a log,
# hundreds of MB each, and every one is regenerable from the command in its own
# log header. The MEASUREMENTS off them live in the driver banner and in
# doc/m3lb.tex, which is where a number belongs.
run_rb*/
run_csf3_*/

# Build trees from this session (build_th, build_th32). Same rule as above --
# the pattern only matched build/ and build-*/.
build_*/

# Imported city geometry and the OSM responses it was built from. Regenerable
# by one command (see "Importing a city from OpenStreetMap" in CLAUDE.md), and
# the cache alone is 4.4 MB for Manchester. What pins a raster is not this
# directory but the lat/lon and the cached response recorded in its meta.json:
# the same place STRING geocodes 1.5 km differently seven weeks apart.
geom/

# demonstrator/tg_mhd writes one directory per run, one level deeper than the
# rules above reach: results/P_tg_mhd/<run>/{anim_frames,vti,raw}/. Frames,
# ParaView files and the full float32 volumes are all regenerable from the case;
# the run's series.dat is the reference data and stays tracked. `**` because the
# CSF3 ladder comes back one level deeper again, results/P_tg_mhd/ladder/<run>/.
results/P_tg_mhd/**/anim_frames/
results/P_tg_mhd/**/vti/
results/P_tg_mhd/**/raw/
# tools/tg_mhd_slices.py's planes of those volumes, ~100 MB a run: regenerable
# from raw/, and the figures made from them are tracked in results/P_tg_mhd/fig/.
results/P_tg_mhd/**/slices/
results/P_tg_mhd/**/png/
results/P_tg_mhd/**/*.mp4
